      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Originally named Camp Colorado, the military post was built on the east bank of the Colorado River to protect emigrants and wagon trains.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/arizona_village_arizona",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Arizona Village sits within the Fort Mojave Indian Reservation, a sovereign territory shared by Arizona, California, and Nevada.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/arizona_village_arizona",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The Colorado River borders Arizona Village to the southwest, offering residents scenic views and recreational opportunities.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        24
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/arizona_village_arizona",
      "category": "nature",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Utilities in Arizona Village are managed by the Fort Mojave Tribal Utilities Authority, reflecting the reservation's emphasis on community services.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        5
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
